                                    2. Dyck factors and the literal map
   A Dyck path of semilength n is a word in U, D with n occurrences of each letter such that every prefix
contains at least as many U ’s as D’s. Let Dn denote the set of such paths. A nonempty Dyck path is
primitive if its only return to height zero occurs after its final step. Cutting at all positive returns gives a
unique factorisation
(1)                                                    P = C1 C2 · · · Ck
into primitive Dyck paths. We write κ(P ) = k.
    Every primitive first factor has a unique first-return form C1 = U AD with A a possibly empty Dyck
path. Define
