                                       3. The factor-count clock
  The first primitive component absorbs exactly one following component per round. The next lemma
makes that statement precise at every time.
Lemma 3.1 (Closed orbit). Let P = C1 · · · Ck , where C1 = U AD. For every 0 ≤ t ≤ k − 1,
(10)                                Φt (P ) = U A C2 · · · Ct+1 D Ct+2 · · · Ck .
When t = 0, the block C2 · · · Ct+1 is empty; when t = k − 1, the suffix Ct+2 · · · Ck is empty.
Proof. At t = 0, (10) reads U ADC2 · · · Ck = P . Suppose the formula holds for some t < k − 1. The word
                                                U A C2 · · · Ct+1 D
is primitive: after its initial U , the Dyck word AC2 · · · Ct+1 stays at or above height one, and the displayed
D is its first return to height zero. Hence the primitive factorisation of the right-hand side of (10) consists
of that first word, followed by Ct+2 , . . . , Ck . Applying (2) moves the closing D across Ct+2 and gives
                                      U A C2 · · · Ct+1 Ct+2 D Ct+3 · · · Ck ,
which is (10) at time t + 1. Induction proves the claim.                                                      □
Corollary 3.2 (Recurrent set and pointwise clock). The statements in theorem 2.1(i)–(ii) hold.
Proof. For t < k − 1, the factorisation displayed in the proof of lemma 3.1 has one first component and
k − t − 1 untouched components, for a total of k − t primitive factors. Thus each nonfixed step decreases
κ by one. At t = k − 1, the formula is the single primitive path
(11)                                        E(P ) = U AC2 · · · Ck D.
It is fixed, so τ (P ) = k − 1.
   If k = 1, the definition fixes P . If k > 1, the strict decrease of κ prevents P from lying on a cycle.
Fixed and recurrent paths are therefore exactly the primitive paths. Deleting the first U and last D is a
bijection from primitive paths in Dn to Dn−1 , giving Catn−1 fixed paths.
   Every primitive factor has semilength at least one, so k ≤ n. Equality holds only when all k factors
have semilength one, and the only such factor is U D. In view of (5), the depth is at most n − 1, with
equality exactly at (U D)n .                                                                           □
   The proof gives more than a global bound: the component statistic is the exact amount of time remaining
at every state. In particular, no transient can merge with another transient of a different factor count before
their depths agree.

                                     4. Complete temporal layers
   The clock reduces temporal enumeration to a component census. We include the coefficient calculation
to fix the boundary case and normalisation, while treating the enumerative machinery as background.
